{
  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Non-standard wording is not, by itself, ruled out: regulation 4.4 requires listed terms only as far as practicable and otherwise requires sufficient information for the Registrar to determine classification.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMR1995/r4.4",
          "quote": "(6) The goods and/or services must, as far as practicable, be specified in terms appearing in any listing of goods and services that is: (a) published by the Registrar; and (b) made available for inspection by the public at the Trade Marks Office and its sub-offices (if any)."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMR1995/r4.4",
          "quote": "(7) If any of the goods and/or services cannot be specified using terms referred to in subregulation (6), the applicant must provide sufficient information to enable the Registrar to decide the classification of the goods and/or services."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "For an international registration designating Australia (IRDA), the Manual states that the goods and services must accord with the Nice Classification system and that the International Bureau (IB) checks classification before transmission to IP Australia.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part60/4/6/4/6/2",
          "quote": "The goods and/or services claimed in an IRDA must accord with the Nice Classification system"
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part60/4/6/4/6/2",
          "quote": "The goods and/or services must comply with the Nice Classification System and will have been checked, and amended if necessary, by the IB prior to transmission of the international registration to IP Australia."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual nevertheless permits an Australian provisional refusal for vague, incomprehensible or linguistically incorrect terms, asking the holder to explain or limit the claim.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part60/4/6/4/6/2",
          "quote": "In the case of claims that have vague terms, are incomprehensible or linguistically incorrect, a provisional refusal may be issued, and the holder will be asked to explain or limit the claim."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Under the Manual's general examination practice, where wording is too vague or unclear to classify, the examiner should request directly relevant website information, trade brochures or pamphlets rather than suggest adding classes at that stage.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part14/4/4/6/2",
          "quote": "If part or all of the specification is vague or unclear to the extent that it cannot be classified, the Examiner should request further information from the applicant. It should not be suggested to the applicant at this stage that they may be able to add a class or classes. The request should be for further information in the form of information from the applicant's website, or trade brochures or pamphlets published by the applicant, which are directly relevant to the application (reg 4.4(7))."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Any explanation should address what the wording actually means, because the Manual construes the claim by its ordinary meaning rather than the applicant's subjective intentions.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part14/7/7/1",
          "quote": "This means that it will be the ordinary meaning conveyed by the wording of the claim itself rather than the applicant’s subjective intentions that determines the scope of a claim"
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "If limiting the specification is necessary, the Manual places responsibility for proposing the limitation on the applicant, while allowing examiners to assist with exclusion wording once the area of business interest is defined.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part27/3/3/2~2",
          "quote": "It is the applicant’s responsibility to propose any amendments which result in limiting the specification as only the applicant knows the exact area of business interest within which the trade mark is or will be used. Examiners may assist with the wording of a proposed exclusion statement once the area of interest is defined."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual states that, if the holder does not respond to the request to explain or limit the problematic terms, those terms will be refused.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part60/4/6/4/6/2",
          "quote": "If there is no response from the holder, the terms will be refused (see 4.14 Acceptance and 4.18 Protection)."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The international-specific guidance supplied concerns IRDAs; these passages do not establish the complete procedure for correcting wording in an outgoing international application filed through Australia as Office of origin.",
      "quotes": [],
      "authority": "none"
    }
  ],
  "declined": false,
